  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 159 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging will continue to dominate the early portion of \nthe long term forecast period until it starts to breakdown Friday \ninto Saturday. This ridge will start to intensify on Thursday with \nnorthwesterly flow moving in aloft. With northwesterly flow also \nanticipated at 500mb, multiple vorticity maxima will move around the \n500mb high over the Four Corners and push across the region from the \nnorthwest. This will induce some forcing for ascent across the CWA. \nThe 700mb flow will be messy at best on Thursday with ridging to \nstart the day before a shortwave pushes through and turns winds out \nof the south and southeast for locations east of the Laramie Range. \nModest 700mb warm air advection will be possible as the shortwave \npushes into the CWA, with the strongest WAA possible in and around \nthe South Laramie Range from Laramie to Cheyenne. 700mb temperatures \nwill warm into the 13 to 15C range Thursday afternoon, with \nsoutheasterly winds east of the Laramie Range favoring upslope \ndevelopment of the South Laramie Range. Despite warming 700mb \ntemperatures, surface temperatures are anticipated to remain in the \nlow-80s to low-90s Thursday afternoon, with moisture starting to \nfunnel in from the south due to the southerly flow. GFS soundings \nsuggest modest CAPE possible, though potentially capped, in the \nafternoons, around 1000 J/kg. Soundings also suggest decent turning \nwith height, and around 35 to 40 kts of 0-6km shear. This would be \nenough to get a few isolated strong storms, if they can overcome the \ncap.\n\nA similar setup is expected for Friday as the upper-level ridge \nremains overhead, but starts to flatten out Friday night into \nSaturday morning. Westerly flow is expected at 500mb as the ridge \nweakens with the peak starting to flatten out. A few lobes of 500mb \nvorticity look to advect through the 500mb flow and into the region, \nthough the strength looks much weaker than the lobes passing through \non Thursday, but the overall coverage increases a touch. Therefore, \nmore widespread, weak, synoptic forcing for ascent should develop \nacross much of the region Friday afternoon. Westerly 700mb flow is \nalso expected as the shortwave moves across the region ahead of the \nnext incoming shortwave expected for Saturday. 700mb height \ngradients will increase, leading to a stronger, westerly jet around \n35kts by Friday evening. WAA will increase Friday evening as \ndownsloping along the Laramie Range increases with the 700mb jet \noverhead. Surface temperatures on Friday are anticipated to be hot \nonce more, with highs in the mid-80s to upper-90s across much of the \narea. Despite synoptic forcing for ascent and increasing 700mb \ntemperatures, precipitation will be hard to get, largely due to \ndownslope drying off the Laramie Range and out into the Plains. Kept \nlow PoPs due to some uncertainty in exactly how much precipitation \nwill be possible, but organized convection appears unlikely at this \ntime.\n\nSaturday onwards becomes much more active across the region as an \nupper-level trough starts to dig south out of Canada into Montana \nand North Dakota. Strong, southwesterly flow will develop between \nthe trough to the north and the ridge to the south, placing the CWA \nin a region of strong, southwesterly flow aloft. The 500mb high over \nthe Four Corners Region will push southeastward as the ridge begins \nto interact with the incoming trough, leading to west-southwesterly \nflow at 500mb Saturday through the start of the week. Multiple lobes \nof 500mb vorticity will push through daily across the region, \nfavoring increased precipitation chances throughout the weekend into \nthe early half of the work week. Looking down at 700mb, a shortwave \nis progged to push through the region Saturday. 700mb height \ngradients increase and result in a jet around 40kts through the \nearly morning into the midday timeframe. A surface low develops \nSaturday morning and pushes off to the northeast, increasing surface \npressure gradients across the region. GFS downward omega values \nalong the Laramie Range will be quite high, leading to increased \nconcern for strong, downsloping winds Saturday morning. High Winds \nare not anticipated at this time, but the setup looks favorable and \nwill continue to be monitored. The remainder of the long term will \nsee daily precipitation chances and seasonable temperatures across \nthe region.",
